<div class="learn-box">
**What you will learn**

Choose who enters a study, define episodes and observation coverage, reuse a cohort, identify new users and sample controls at case time.
</div>

**You need:** the client packages and the Opal login from
[Getting started](book-00-start.html). This chapter uses `omop.questionnaire`,
the synthetic questionnaire study described there.

## Start this chapter

Log in first:

```{r book-local-server, include = FALSE}
# Outputs come from a local DataSHIELD server holding the same resources as the Opal in the text.
library(dsOMOPClient)
source("tools/book/display.R")
source("tools/book/connection.R")
```

```{r chapter-setup, eval = FALSE}
library(dsOMOPClient)
conns <- ds.omop.login("https://localhost:8443", user = "administrator",
  password = "password", resource = "omop.questionnaire", server = "site")$conns
```

```{r chapter-setup-local-2, include = FALSE}
conns <- book_connect("questionnaire")$conns
```

The connection is ready; `conns` identifies the server used by the analysis calls.

The 48 questionnaire participants form four groups. This short description
lets you predict membership before running a recipe.

| People | Visits per person | Recorded death | Education answers |
|---:|---|---|---|
| 12 | Outpatient, then inpatient | Myocardial infarction | Secondary → University |
| 12 | Outpatient, then inpatient | Pneumonia | University → Secondary |
| 12 | One outpatient | Myocardial infarction | None |
| 12 | None | None | None |

Observation runs from 2019 through 2025. Half of each death group died in 2023,
the other half in 2024. [Chapter 4](book-04-vocabulary.html) identifies Inpatient
Visit as **9201** and Outpatient Visit as **9202**.

## Start with an index event

A *population* specifies who contributes rows. An *index* selects an event date
for each study episode. Here a person's first visit starts one episode.

```{r index}
visits <- omop_event("visit_occurrence")
visitors <- omop_population("visitors", index = omop_index(visits, select = "first"))
```

People without visits are absent. The default episode end is the end of the
covering observation period. `select = "all"` would keep recurrent visit episodes.

## Release membership as a private table

```{r membership}
visit_members <- omop_recipe(populations = list(visitors),
  outputs = omop_output("members", rows = "visitors", format = "membership"))
recipe_execute(visit_members, out = c(members = "D_members"), conns = conns)
```

Membership keeps private keys and episode dates. Count its rows:

```{r member-columns}
dsBaseClient::ds.dim("D_members", datasources = conns)
```

There are 36 rows: one first-visit episode for each visitor. The 12 people
without visits do not enter. Later, selecting every visit will make a row count
different from a person count.

## Require an eligible visit

A *criterion* is an inclusion test. These criteria require an inpatient record
on the index day and at least 365 days of observation before it.

```{r criteria}
inpatient <- omop_event("visit_occurrence", concepts = 9201L)
eligible <- omop_population("eligible",
  index = omop_index(visits, select = "first_eligible"),
  filters = list(omop_observed(before = 365),
    omop_has(inpatient, window = omop_window(0, 0))))
```

`first_eligible` tests the complete criterion tree at each candidate, then selects
the earliest passing visit. `first` selects a person's first visit before testing;
it would not advance to a later visit when that first one fails.
Combine alternatives with `omop_any()` and joint requirements with `omop_all()`.
Only TRUE survives; an unknown criterion is not evidence of eligibility.

```{r eligible-recipe}
eligible_recipe <- omop_recipe(populations = list(eligible),
  variables = list(visits = omop_count("visit_occurrence", before = Inf)),
  outputs = omop_output("people", rows = "eligible", format = "wide"))
recipe_execute(eligible_recipe, out = c(people = "D_eligible"), conns = conns)
```

The count includes visits through the chosen index day.

```{r eligible-result}
dsBaseClient::ds.dim("D_eligible", datasources = conns)
dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_eligible$visits",
  useNA = "no", datasources = conns)$output.list$TABLES.COMBINED_all.sources_counts
```

There are 24 eligible people, each with two visits through entry. Their first
visit was outpatient; their second visit is the first eligible inpatient visit.
All have sufficient prior observation. The other 12 visitors never have an
inpatient visit, so they do not qualify.

## Make coverage explicit

An *observation period* states when the data source observes a person. The default
requires exactly one period covering the index and drops missing or ambiguous
coverage. A union policy can join adjacent periods when that matches the study.

For this dataset every person has one covering period, so a union policy would
not change membership. In a resource with adjacent observation periods, declare
`coverage = omop_coverage(policy = "union", gap = 0)` in `omop_population()`.
A zero gap allows no unobserved days. Observation coverage does not establish
that someone attended a visit or took a medicine.

## Reuse an existing cohort

A *results cohort* is a named definition with private membership and start/end dates
already available to the resource. Saved cohort 1 contains the 24 people in the two groups
with myocardial infarction deaths. Its ID is a definition ID, not a patient ID.

```{r cohort}
cohort_population <- omop_population("saved", index = omop_index(
  list(cohort_definition_id = 1L), select = "all", end = omop_end("event_end")))
cohort_recipe <- omop_recipe(populations = list(cohort_population),
  outputs = omop_output("members", rows = "saved", format = "membership"))
recipe_execute(cohort_recipe, out = c(members = "D_cohort"), conns = conns)
dsBaseClient::ds.dim("D_cohort", datasources = conns)
```

The 24 rows are the saved cohort episodes. This reads existing membership
without writing a cohort to the database.
Use `omop_in_cohort(1L)` as a criterion when you want membership alone while
retaining a different index. Do not invent a cohort ID on a real resource.

## Identify new users

A *washout* requires an observed period without the study exposure before entry.
An *era* joins nearby exposure intervals. The shortcut combines drug records
into eras, selects the first eligible era,
and checks both observation and absence of prior exposure.

The medicine here is **950001**, Questionnaire study medicine. Look it up
before selecting it:

```{r medicine-id}
medicine <- ds.omop.concept.lookup(950001L, conns = conns)
medicine$pooled[c("concept_id", "concept_name")]
```

This is a teaching medicine concept. We will group its records by concept ID.

```{r new-users}
new_users <- omop_new_users(950001L, washout_days = 365, by = "concept")
new_user_recipe <- omop_study(new_users, outputs = "baseline",
  baseline = list(prior_fills = omop_count("drug_exposure", 950001L,
    window = omop_window(-365, -1))))
recipe_execute(new_user_recipe, out = c(baseline = "D_new_users"), conns = conns)
```

The synthetic medicine uses concept grouping explicitly; ingredient grouping is
the normal default when the vocabulary supplies ingredient relationships.
The era uses a 30-day gap and ends at the treatment interval end.
“New user” means no recorded use during this washout, not no lifetime use.

```{r new-users-result}
dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_new_users$prior_fills",
  useNA = "no", datasources = conns)$output.list$TABLES.COMBINED_all.sources_counts
```

All 36 new users have zero prior fills in days −365 to −1. That is an
eligibility requirement, not a result to discover after admitting people.

## Define cases and the control pool

*Risk-set sampling* selects controls who are still eligible at each case's event
time. A person who becomes a case later may serve as a control earlier.
For a compact teaching example the outcome is death.

```{r case-control-pools}
outcome <- omop_event("death")
cases <- omop_population("cases", index = omop_index(outcome),
  filters = list(omop_observed(before = 365)))
controls <- omop_population("controls",
  filters = list(omop_observed(before = 365)))
```

The control criteria are evaluated at the case's date. The sampler also checks
that a control is alive and has not had the outcome on or before that date.

## Match at case time

```{r match}
matched <- omop_population("matched", match = omop_match(
  cases = "cases", controls = "controls", outcome = outcome,
  match = list("sex"), controls_per_case = 1L, seed = 42L,
  prior_outcome = "exclude", future_cases = "eligible",
  replacement = "between_sets", insufficient = "keep_available"))
```

The seed fixes the sampling order. Controls may be reused between sets here;
`replacement = "none"` forbids that reuse. If too few controls are available,
this policy keeps those available; `drop_case` would discard that case's set.
Here controls must share the case's sex. Risk-set sampling already places
each control at the case date, so matching calendar year would add nothing.
Other designs may add age bands or prior-event strata.

```{r matched-recipe}
matched_recipe <- omop_recipe(populations = list(cases, controls, matched),
  variables = list(is_case = omop_population_column("is_case"),
    set = omop_population_column("set_id"), sex = omop_sex()),
  outputs = omop_output("sample", rows = "matched", format = "wide"))
recipe_execute(matched_recipe, out = c(sample = "D_matched"), conns = conns)
```

The `set` column is a protected match-set key. Keep it for analyses that account
for matching, such as conditional logistic regression; it is not a covariate to
copy or summarise.

```{r matched-result}
dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_matched$is_case", "D_matched$sex",
  useNA = "no", datasources = conns)$output.list$TABLES.COMBINED_all.sources_counts
```

Rows 1 and 0 represent cases and controls. Each has 14 females and 22 males:
36 case rows and 36 control rows in total. Controls can recur
between sets, so these are sampled rows, not 72 different people. They are
not disease prevalence or counts of unique people. Matching defines a design;
it does not by itself remove all confounding.

## Exercise 1: count every visit episode

Keep every visit instead of only the first. Compare the row count with the
36-person visitor roster above. Could you sum the resulting episode durations
as person-time?

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```{r solution}
all_visitors <- omop_population("visitors", index = omop_index(visits, select = "all"))
all_recipe <- omop_recipe(populations = list(all_visitors),
  outputs = omop_output("members", rows = "visitors", format = "membership"))
recipe_execute(all_recipe, out = c(members = "D_all_visitors"), conns = conns)
dsBaseClient::ds.dim("D_all_visitors", datasources = conns)
```

There are 60 episodes from 36 visitors: 24 people have two visits and 12 have one.
With the default end at observation end, episodes for the same person overlap;
summing their durations would count time repeatedly. Choose an appropriate
`omop_end("event_end")` or `omop_end("fixed", ...)` and resolve overlaps before
using episodes to measure person-time.

</details>

## Exercise 2: first versus first eligible

Use `select = "first"` with the same inpatient and prior-observation criteria.
Would it advance past an outpatient first visit?

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```{r first-refusal, warning=FALSE, error = TRUE}
first_only <- omop_population("first_only",
  index = omop_index(visits, select = "first"),
  filters = list(omop_observed(before = 365),
    omop_has(inpatient, window = omop_window(0, 0))))
first_recipe <- omop_recipe(populations = list(first_only),
  outputs = omop_output("members", rows = "first_only", format = "membership"))
recipe_execute(first_recipe, out = c(members = "D_first_only"), conns = conns)
```

Every first visit in the teaching data is outpatient, so nobody meets the
inpatient criterion at that visit. The empty result is refused; the message
itself does not disclose a count. `first_eligible` searches later visits and
admits the 24 people shown earlier. This is a different study definition,
not a way to weaken the release rule.

</details>
## Recap

Index selection sets episodes; criteria and coverage decide eligibility. Existing cohorts, new-user eras and risk-set samples are population definitions in the same language.

**Next:** [8. Choosing an output format](book-08-outputs.html).
